{"id":"dbb6ea38-cea9-49ca-bdb6-97c37f6ea7b8","object":"chat.completion","created":1784807136,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get('payload')\\n    if payload is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing payload')\\n    tokens = payload.get('tokens')\\n    calls = payload.get('calls')\\n    if tokens is None or calls is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing tokens or calls')\\n    if not isinstance(tokens, list):\\n        raise ValueError('tokens must be a list')\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError('calls must be a list')\\n    # Validate each call\\n    for call in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError('Each call must be a dict')\\n        for key in ('call_id', 'start', 'end', 'score', 'replacement_tokens'):\\n            if key not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Missing key {key} in call')\\n        start = call['start']\\n        end = call['end']\\n        score = call['score']\\n        if not isinstance(start, int):\\n            raise ValueError('start must be an integer')\\n        if not isinstance(end, int):\\n            raise ValueError('end must be an integer')\\n        if not (0 <= start < end <= len(tokens)):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid start/end indices')\\n        if not isinstance(score, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError('score must be a number')\\n        if not isinstance(call['replacement_tokens'], list):\\n            raise ValueError('replacement_tokens must be a list')\\n        for rt in call['replacement_tokens']:\\n            if not isinstance(rt, str):\\n                raise ValueError('Each replacement token must be a string')\\n    # Sort calls by descending score, then ascending start, then call_id\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\\n    selected = []\\n    # Track intervals as (start, end) in order of start\\n    intervals = []\\n    for call in sorted_calls:\\n        start = call['start']\\n        end = call['end']\\n        overlapping = False\\n        for s, e in intervals:\\n            if not (end <= s or start >= e):\\n                overlapping = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlapping:\\n            selected.append(call)\\n            intervals.append((start, end))\\n    # Sort selected by start ascending for output\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c['start'])\\n    selected_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]\\n    # Build interleaved tokens: replace from right to left\\n    interleaved = list(tokens)\\n    # sort by start descending for replacement\\n    for call in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c['start'], reverse=True):\\n        start = call['start']\\n        end = call['end']\\n        replacement = call['replacement_tokens']\\n        interleaved = interleaved[:start] + replacement + interleaved[end:]\\n    return {\\n        'selected_ids': selected_ids,\\n        'interleaved_tokens': interleaved\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns JSON-serializable object. The case has payload with tokens list and calls list. Each call has call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. The objective: Accept calls that do not overlap, ranking by descending score, then start (ascending? careful: \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" Typically descending score, then ascending start? They say \"start position\" without specifying order, likely ascending because \"then call ID\" is likely ascending. But the phrase \"descending score, start position, then call ID\" ambiguous. Usually ranking is first by score descending, then by start position ascending (since earlier start is better?), then by call ID ascending. But we need to check \"Greedily reject any span overlapping an already selected span.\" So we sort calls by the ranking criteria, then iterate, accept if no overlap with already accepted.\n\nAfter selecting accepted calls, order them by source start for reporting (ascending start). Then apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift. That means we process accepted calls in descending order of start? Actually \"Apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift.\" This is typical: if you replace tokens from left to right, the indices of subsequent tokens shift because the string length changes. Doing from right to left (i.e., decreasing start) avoids that. So we should sort accepted calls by start descending, then apply replacement: replace tokens[start:end] with replacement_tokens.\n\nThen produce final interleaved token stream: The original tokens with those replacements. Also return selected IDs (list of call_id in order of source start ascending? The description says \"Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\" Also \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" So we output selected_ids in order of start ascending. And interleaved_tokens as a list of strings.\n\nAdditionally, we must validate every required field. Raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. So we need to check that all fields exist, types are correct, indices within bounds, etc.\n\nThe problem statement also mentions \"accepted_call_interleaving\" and the function name is solve, taking case (a dict with payload). Return a dict with keys? Based on description: \"Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\" Also need aggregate values? It says \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision, and compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" But the specific output format? Looking at the fixture, it's just input shapes. The output is not specified in the fixture. We have to infer from the task: It says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, implementation.\" That's for the solution submission. But the solve function itself should return a JSON-serializable object. Likely we need to return a dict with keys: \"selected_ids\" (list of call_id strings), \"interleaved_tokens\" (list of strings). Possibly also \"calls\" (the accepted calls with their properties)? But the instruction says \"Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\" So likely that's all.\n\nBut also \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" might mean include call_id, start, end, score? Maybe the output should contain the accepted calls with their details? However the instruction says \"Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\" So we'll return {\"selected_ids\": [...], \"interleaved_tokens\": [...]}. Or maybe also \"accepted_calls\" with full info? The phrase \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" could be interpreted as we should include the original call details for the accepted calls? But the output is from solve, which is used by the framework. We need to match expected output format from the problem statement? Let's read the task more carefully: \"Apply accepted API calls to text while preserving registered span order, rejecting overlaps, and keeping all eligible calls. ... Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\" So return two fields.\n\nBut also \"compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" That suggests the output should be the primitive decisions, i.e., the list of selected calls and the final tokens. So we'll output that.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Extract payload from case (case is dict with key \"payload\"? Actually the case structure from fixture: each case has \"case_id\" and \"payload\". So we need to access case[\"payload\"].\n2. Validate payload: must have \"tokens\" (list), \"calls\" (list). For each call: check presence of \"call_id\", \"start\", \"end\", \"score\", \"replacement_tokens\". \"start\" and \"end\" should be ints, within [0, len(tokens)], start < end. \"score\" numeric (float). \"replacement_tokens\" list of strings. Also ensure no overlapping? Not needed for validation, but we handle in selection.\n3. Sort calls by (descending score, ascending start, ascending call_id). Note: call_id is string, compare lexicographically.\n4. Initialize selected = [] list of accepted call dicts.\n5. Maintain a set of occupied intervals? Since tokens are discrete indices, we can have a list of booleans or a sorted list of intervals. For simplicity, we can maintain a list of accepted intervals and check overlap by comparing start/end.\n6. For each call in sorted order: check if its interval [start, end) overlaps with any already selected interval. If not, add to selected.\n7. After selection, sort selected by start ascending for reporting (for selected_ids) and also for replacement order (we need descending for replacement, but we'll sort separately).\n8. For replacement: create a list of tokens (copy of tokens). Then sort selected by start descending. For each call in that order: replace tokens[start:end] with replacement_tokens list. This changes token list length. We need to carefully slice and assign. Since we are in Python, we can do: tokens = tokens[:start] + replacement_tokens + tokens[end:]. But if we do in descending start order, the indices remain valid because we haven't shifted earlier parts. However, when we replace, the length changes, but later replacements (which are earlier in the original order) will have their start/end indices based on original tokens? Actually if we replace from right to left, the left intervals' indices are unaffected because we only modify positions to the right. So we can just use the original start/end. That works.\n9. Flatten the result: interleaved_tokens list.\n10. Return dict with \"selected_ids\" (list of call_id in ascending start order) and \"interleaved_tokens\".\n\nEdge cases: No calls selected -> selected_ids empty, interleaved_tokens = tokens copy.\nInvalid case: raise ValueError.\n\nWe need to ensure no external imports, only standard library.\n\nLet's write the code as a string.\n\nBut the output must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string (escaped). So we construct that.\n\nWe'll write solve function with proper error handling.\n\nLet's produce the JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1339,"completion_tokens":2259,"total_tokens":3598,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1517},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1280,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":59},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}